<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cornelis de Houtman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1565">1565</a>–<a href="/years/1599">1599</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cornelis de Houtman was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> explorer who led the first Dutch expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, which sailed in 1595. Although only about a third of the crew survived, the voyage proved the route commercially viable. It triggered a scramble among Dutch trading companies that led to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cornelis-de-houtman.jpg" alt="Cornelis de Houtman" width="247" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:26_Cornelis_de_Houtman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cornelis de Houtman was born on 2 April 1565 in Gouda, South Holland, the son of the brewer Pieter de Houtman. His younger brother Frederick was born in 1571. In 1592 his wealthy cousin Reynier Pauw and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> merchants formed the Compagnie van Verre to finance a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trading expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, inspired by maps of the route supplied by the cartographer Petrus Plancius. That year Pauw sent Houtman and his brother to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. It is unclear whether he had real business there or went chiefly as a commercial spy on the Portuguese spice trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 2 April 1595 four ships left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> under Houtman: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, Amsterdam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapura">Hollandia</a> and Duifje. Scurvy broke out after a few weeks, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> seventy-one of the 248 sailors had to be buried. Quarrels broke out among the captains and traders after a skipper died. The ships reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, in June 1596, passing through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda Strait</a> rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Malacca Strait</a>, which the Portuguese controlled, as Jan Huyghen van Linschoten had advised.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> entered a friendly treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, but Houtman was insulting to him and was turned away for rude behaviour without buying any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>. Attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, he ordered his men to attack and rape the civilian population there in revenge. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> the ships obtained a few pots of peppercorns on 26 February 1597. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> was deliberately burned at Bawean, the crew decided against continuing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, and another skipper died, with Houtman accused of poisoning him. Portuguese ships kept the fleet from taking on water at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a>, and only 87 men returned, too weak to moor the ships themselves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The voyage was a humanitarian disaster and probably only broke even, but it showed that the Portuguese monopoly on the spice trade was vulnerable. Within five years 65 more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ships had sailed east, and the Dutch eventually displaced the Portuguese and took over the spice trade. The voyage may be regarded as the start of Dutch colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p><p>Houtman made a second journey east in 1599 for a different company and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, where the sultan received him peacefully until Houtman insulted him. He was defeated by Admiral Keumalahayati and her Inong Balee army, and died on 11 September 1599.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cornelis de Houtman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Philip II, and within fourteen years Dutch merchants had sent the first expedition to the East Indies under <b>Cornelis de Houtman</b> (1595–1597); only about a third of the original crews survived, but the voyage proved the route commercially viable and set off a scramble among rival Dutch trading…</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch sailors under <b>Cornelis de Houtman</b> visited in 1597.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gelgel">Kingdom of Gelgel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gouda, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in the Aceh Sultanate. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.02,4.71&amp;year=1587" aria-label="Gouda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4656 366 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cornelis de Houtman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5106 636h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5106 636h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.02,4.71&amp;year=1587">What was here in 1587? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
